                    coach_default:          `Move logged.`,
                    coach_r1_beneath:       `<strong>You're reading the right room.</strong> The 78% is polite compliance — the same surface support the three dead programmes had. Pricing in low actual readiness means you'll design for how this organisation behaves, not how the survey wishes it did.`,
                    coach_r1_moderate:      `<strong>Closer, but generous.</strong> “Pockets of resistance” undersells it — the resistance here is structural and quiet, not a few hold-outs. The barons who never answer surveys honestly are the ones who buried the last three.`,
                    coach_r1_surveytrust:   `<strong>That's exactly how programmes one, two and three started.</strong> Your survey says 78% support, so you've declared readiness high and moved to execution. All three predecessors also had high survey support. What is the 78% hiding, and where are the barons who never answer surveys honestly?`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> Low real readiness <em>and</em> the psychodynamic dimension named as the binding constraint. The pull back to the old ways isn't a process problem — it's anxiety, defensiveness and threat. That's where changeability is built.`,
                    coach_r1_gapsmall:      `<strong>You've under-priced the gap.</strong> A small stated-minus-actual gap assumes the 78% is mostly real. Three buried programmes say otherwise — the gap between what people say and what they'll do is where this dies.`,
                    coach_r2_status:        `<strong>That's the root.</strong> The new operating model erodes the status and informal control the barons spent 20 years building. Their resistance is rational — and until you address what they're actually losing, no comms campaign will move it.`,
                    coach_r2_competence:    `<strong>Partly right.</strong> Competence fear is real, but it's secondary. The barons aren't mainly afraid they can't lead digitally — they're losing the informal power the new model strips out. Treat the status threat or the resistance stays.`,
                    coach_r2_irrational:    `<strong>You've labelled them “dinosaurs.”</strong> But their resistance is rational — the new model erodes the status and control they built over 20 years. Until you address what they're actually losing, your contempt just hardens them. What's legitimate in their resistance?`,
                    coach_r2_mapped:        `<strong>You mapped the lethal resistance.</strong> The quiet barons with informal power — not the noisy works-council critics — are what buries programmes. Noisy-but-harmless dissent is visible; quiet-but-lethal reversion is what you just located.`,
                    coach_r2_vocal:         `<strong>You're chasing the noise.</strong> The vocal critics make the most sound and the least difference. The lethal resistance is quiet — the barons who agree in the meeting and brief against you afterward.`,
                    coach_r2_contempt:      `<strong>Override mode is how you harden them.</strong> Treating resistance as pure obstruction throws away the information it carries — and tells the barons their experience counts for nothing. Resistance is data; listen before you push.`,
                    coach_r3_coopt:         `<strong>You worked with informal power instead of crushing it.</strong> Giving the Division Head visible ownership of something she values turns the most dangerous blocker into a sponsor — and signals to every baron that the change has room for them.`,
                    coach_r3_remove:        `<strong>You've sidelined the Division Head.</strong> But she controls the branch network you need to execute through — and removing her tells every baron they're next. Now they all dig in. You can't crush informal power; you have to work with it.`,
                    coach_r3_bypass:        `<strong>Bypassing isn't co-opting.</strong> Executing around the branch network avoids the fight but leaves the barons intact and threatened — they'll quietly starve the new channels of the people and data they need.`,
                    coach_r3_symptom:       `<strong>You treated the symptom.</strong> Town halls and confrontation move the engagement numbers, but claims digitisation still stalls because the barons quietly tell their teams to “wait and see.” You haven't touched the psychodynamic root. What threat is actually driving them?`,
                    coach_r3_confront:      `<strong>Public confrontation backfired.</strong> Naming the covert blockers without first co-opting or addressing their threat hands them a grievance and a cause — and spends political capital you'll want at the checkpoint.`,
                    coach_r3_balanced:      `<strong>A coherent portfolio.</strong> Co-opting the Division Head, treating the root threat and engineering an early win — you're building changeability and credibility at once, not just decorating the surface.`,
                    coach_r4_phased:        `<strong>You sequenced instead of detonating.</strong> Landing one workstream first builds the credibility and capacity to do the hard parts. The organisation absorbs the change rather than snapping back.`,
                    coach_r4_bigbang:       `<strong>You detonated.</strong> Claims, back-office and branch redesign all at once — the organisation that killed three programmes is now overloaded across three fronts with no early win to point to. Where's the move that earned you the right to do the hard parts?`,
                    coach_r4_frozen:        `<strong>Avoiding provocation is avoiding the change.</strong> Minimal motion protects you from a fight today and guarantees programme number four. The board funded movement; the clock — 2 points of share a year — is still running.`,
                    coach_r4_hardest:       `<strong>Hardest-first is a credibility gamble.</strong> Branch redesign signals you're serious, but without an early win first you spend capital before you've banked any — and the barons get their evidence that the change “doesn't work.”`,
                    coach_r4_claims:        `<strong>Claims is the prize, not the opener.</strong> It's the biggest margin win and the hardest to land through resisting branches. Earn the room with a contained win first, then take the prize.`,
                    coach_r4_underspend:    `<strong>Timid.</strong> Leaving most of the €60M uncommitted starves the transformation of momentum — the board reads hesitation, and the barons read permission to wait.`,
                    coach_r5_resistance:    `<strong>You named the real failure.</strong> The plan was sound; what stalled was the hidden resistance you under-treated — the barons quietly telling teams to wait. Rebuild changeability, don't redesign the strategy.`,
                    coach_r5_plan:          `<strong>You're blaming the plan.</strong> The strategy was sound — what failed was execution, specifically the resistance you under-treated. Don't redesign the plan; rebuild the changeability. What will you do differently with the barons now?`,
                    coach_r5_people:        `<strong>Blaming the people repeats the error.</strong> Replacing “change-fatigued” managers tells every survivor they're disposable and proves the barons right that this change is a threat. The problem isn't capability — it's untreated resistance.`,
                    coach_r5_recover:       `<strong>That's the recovery.</strong> Going back to the root — a credible, status-preserving role for the barons in the new model — is what finally lowers the resistance that stalled claims. This is the move the last three programmes never made.`,
                    coach_r5_comms:         `<strong>More comms is more symptom.</strong> Another town-hall push lifts engagement numbers for a fortnight and changes nothing underneath. The barons are still telling teams to wait. Treat the root, not the readout.`,
                    coach_r5_enforce:       `<strong>Enforcement hardens the reversion.</strong> Mandates and deadlines against covert resistance produce compliance theatre — exactly the polite agreement-without-movement that buried the last three. You can't enforce changeability.`,
                    coach_r5_blameplan:     `<strong>The board hears you abandoning a sound strategy.</strong> Asking for a redesign and more time reads as the programme failing — confidence drops and the €60M is now at real risk. Own the execution gap instead.`,
                    coach_r5_optimism:      `<strong>“It's fine” doesn't survive contact with the data.</strong> The board can see claims stalling. Reassurance without a credible diagnosis of what stalled erodes the confidence you need to protect the funding.`,

